   "text": {
    "L1": "A dot picture can show why a number pattern works, not just what the numbers are.",
    "L2": "Here is a common mistake: when a square grows, some people count the shared corner twice.",
    "L3": "A square number has equal rows and columns, like this 5 by 5 picture.",
    "L4": "To make it 6 by 6, add a new row and a new column around the old square.",
    "L5": "The new row and column share one corner dot, so the new dots are 5 plus 5 plus 1: 11.",
    "L6": "Triangular numbers also have a picture: their rows contain 1, 2, 3, and so on dots.",
    "L7": "Two matching triangles fit into a rectangle, so one triangle is half of that rectangle.",
    "L8": "Now use the shared-corner rule to choose how many dots turn 6 by 6 into 7 by 7."
   },
